 "text": "9/29/26, 9:42 AM\nIn the following example, we assume that the demand registers are balanced.\n\nExample 1:Forecasts from previous periods with minimum portions\n\nMR Result O  180 150  80             2? kW\n||] \u2014_\u2014_\n\nMR Reas. |  Y;  P,  Y>            P.\n08  09-1  01-1  09-2                01-2\n200                        kW/Year\n\nPerDem. \u00a7 |\u2014\u2014VWT HA\n\n40%\nWght Port. |------------------------------\n\nOn installation of the device, a period demand value of 200 kWh/year is entered for the register.\nThe minimum portion of weighting in percent is 30%.\nThe sum of the weighting units for one year is 1000.\n\nWith a weighting portion of 40%, period 09-2 to 08 is representative. This means that extrapolation is based on meter reading\nresults rather than on the period demand.\n\n1. Determination of the highest meter reading from previous reading 09-2 over a representative interval (09-2 to 08):\n\n180 kw\n2. Determined demand = expected demand:\n\n180 kW\n\nThe expected meter reading at time P is 180 kWh.\n\nExample 2:Forecasts from previous periods without minimum portions with period\ndemand\n\nMR Result 0        80                                               2? kW\n| _\u2014__\u2014\u2014\u2014\nMR Reas. |     Y                          P\n08      09                                01\n200                                   kW/Year\nPerDem. \u00a9 |\u2014\u2014-N _\u2014\nWght Unit        200                               800\n\n\\A/nht Part       9no.                            anos\n",
